<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Francis, Duke of Teck</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1837">1837</a>–<a href="/years/1900">1900</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Francis, Duke of Teck, was the son of Duke Alexander of Württemberg and a Hungarian countess; because of this unequal marriage he was excluded from the Württemberg succession. He was created Prince of Teck in 1863 and Duke of Teck in 1871. In 1866 he married Princess Mary Adelaide of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambridge">Cambridge</a> and settled in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, and their daughter Mary became Queen consort of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-v">George V</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/francis-duke-of-teck.jpg" alt="Francis, Duke of Teck" width="320" height="479" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Alexander Bassano · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Prince_Francis,_Duke_of_Teck.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Francis was born on 28 August 1837 in Esseg, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavonia">Slavonia</a> (now <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/osijek">Osijek</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croatia">Croatia</a>), and was known as Count Francis von Hohenstein until 1863. His father, Duke Alexander of Württemberg, was the son of Duke Louis of Württemberg. His mother was the Hungarian Countess Claudine Rhédey de Kis-Rhéde, created Countess von Hohenstein in her own right by Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferdinand-i-of-austria">Ferdinand I of Austria</a>. The marriage was morganatic, so Francis had no succession rights to the Kingdom of Württemberg. He studied at the Imperial and Royal Technical Military Academy from 1849 to 1853 and joined the Imperial Austrian Army as a lieutenant in 1854.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>As an orderly officer under Count von Wimpffen in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> during the Austro-Sardinian War, he won a gold medal for distinguished service at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-solferino">Battle of Solferino</a> in 1859. He served in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austro-prussian-war">Austro-Prussian War</a> and left the Austrian Army when he married and moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> in 1866. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> he held honorary colonelcies of volunteer units, and in 1882 he was attached to the staff of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/garnet-wolseley">Sir Garnet Wolseley</a> in the Egyptian campaign, receiving a medal for the Battle of Tel-el-Kebir. He became a major-general in the British Army in 1893 and a Generalleutnant in the German Army in 1895.</p><p>Because he was the product of a morganatic marriage and had little income, Francis was not an acceptable husband for princesses of most European royal houses. He married Princess Mary Adelaide of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambridge">Cambridge</a>, a daughter of Prince Adolphus, Duke of Cambridge, on 12 June 1866 at St Anne's Church, Kew. They had one daughter and three sons, and the King of Württemberg created him Duke of Teck in 1871.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>The couple lived on Mary Adelaide's Parliamentary allowance of £5,000 a year, with some help from her mother, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/queen-victoria">Queen Victoria</a> refused requests for more. They were given a grace-and-favour apartment in Kensington Palace and White Lodge in Richmond Park, but ran up large debts and in 1883 left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> for the continent, returning in 1885. Queen Victoria granted him the style of Highness on 1 July 1887. Their fortunes improved when their daughter Princess Victoria Mary, known as May, became engaged in 1891 to Prince Albert Victor, Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/glarentza">Clarence</a>; he died six weeks later, and May then accepted a proposal from his brother Prince George, Duke of York.</p><p>After his wife's death in 1897 the duke, always irascible, suffered mental decline, and he died of a stroke on 21 January 1900 at White Lodge. He was buried next to his wife in the Royal Vault at St George's Chapel, Windsor Castle. His daughter became Queen Mary, consort of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-v">George V</a>, and he was the maternal grandfather of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edward-viii">Edward VIII</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-vi">George VI</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Francis, Duke of Teck” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Württemberg later took the ducal title, and in 1871 it was revived for <b>Francis of Teck</b>, father of Queen Mary of Great Britain.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-teck">Duchy of Teck</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/osijek">Osijek</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/croatia">Croatia</a>. Died in White Lodge, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=45.56,18.67&amp;year=1877" aria-label="Osijek on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4993 557 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Francis, Duke of Teck"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5443 827h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5443 827h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=45.56,18.67&amp;year=1877">What was here in 1877? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
